H@L@H@!H@C+H!k1k0-H@.k:k0-H@k1k0-+.H@C+H!k{f@H@!H@C+H!k}f@H@!H@C+H!L!
H@L@H@!H@C+H!k1k0-H@.k H@k1k0-+.H@C+H!k{f@H@!H@C+H!k}f@H@!H@C+H!L!

H@
L@    H@!H@C+H!
k1k0- H@.
k\    H@k1k0-+.H@C+H!
k{f@  H@!H@C+H!
H@
kkf@  H@!H@C+H!
klf@  H@!H@C+H!
k:k0- H@!H@C+H!
k=f@  H@!H@C+H!
kjf@  H@!H@C+H!
      H@!H@C+H!
k}f@  H@!H@C+H!
L!

\ No, my cat did not just walk across my keyboard. :-)

\ What those cryptic characters above did was define some fundamental words:
\ U+000A ( -- ) "line feed" no-op to ignore newline characters
\ U+0020 ( -- ) "space"     no-op to ignore space characters
\ U+005C ( -- ) "backslash" ignore characters until a newline is encountered

\ Why must ignoring whitespace and line comments need to be defined explicitly?
\ Because 0th assumes very little about its execution environment. This keeps
\ the environment simple and portable. When porting to a new system, only the
\ environment needs to be reimplemented. This bootstrap code stays the same.

\ That said, 0th assumes the following properties of its execution environment:
\ - It supports two data sizes: bytes as the smallest addressable unit, and
\   cells large enough to hold addresses and integers.
\ - It provides a contiguous buffer of bytes as workable memory.
\ - It provides two stacks for holding cells:
\     - The parameter stack (pstack) for passing data between words; and
\     - The return stack (rstack) for control flow.
\ - It provides a known set of single-character builtin words that perform
\   operations that cannot be defined in 0th itself. These builtins will be
\   listed below.
\ - It initializes 'here' to point to free memory and 'latest' to point to the
\   most recent builtin. The dictionary, residing in workable memory, already
\   contains all builtins before bootstrap runs.
\ - It interprets this source file by reading bytes, looking up each in the
\   dictionary, and executing its execution token.

\ TODO: Revise builtins

\ # ( -- )                    Halt (breakpoint target for debugging)
\ Q ( n -- )                  Abort execution with error code n
\ C ( -- u )                  Push cell size in bytes
\ H ( -- a-addr )             Push address of 'here' variable
\ L ( -- a-addr )             Push address of 'latest' variable
\ @ ( a-addr -- x )           Fetch cell x from addr
\ $ ( c-addr -- c )           Fetch byte c from addr
\ ! ( x a-addr -- )           Store cell x at addr
\ . ( c c-addr -- )           Store byte c at addr
\ " ( x -- x x )              Duplicate x
\ _ ( x -- )                  Drop x from the stack
\ ~ ( x1 x2 -- x2 x1 )        Swap the top two stack items
\ ^ ( x1 x2 -- x1 x2 x1 )     Place a copy of x1 on top of the stack
\ < ( x1 x2 x3 -- x2 x3 x1 )  Rotate the top three stack entries
\ f ( c -- xt|0 )             Find execution token for c, or 0 if not found
\ j ( ? -- )                  If zero, jump to address in next cell
\ J ( -- )                    Jump to address in next cell
\ k ( -- c )                  Read next character c from the input stream
\ l ( -- x )                  Push literal stored in the next cell
\ x ( xt -- ... )             Execute the XT
\ + ( x1 x2 -- x3 )           Add: x3 = x1 + x2
\ - ( x1 x2 -- x3 )           Subtract: x3 = x1 - x2
\ = ( x1 x2 -- ? )            Equal: -1 if x1 == x2, else 0

\ TODO: Document dictionary structure

\ Lines 4-17 created the "backslash" word before comments existed.
\ Here's what it does in detail:

\ H@                \ save 'here' (this word's address)
\ L@    H@!         \ store 'latest' here
\       H@C+H!      \ increment 'here' by a cell
\ k1k0- H@.         \ store byte 0x1 at 'here' (name length)
\ k\    H@k1k0-+.   \ store byte '\' at ['here' + 1 byte]
\       H@C+H!      \ increment 'here' by a cell
\ k{f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ compile '{' - begin definition
\ H@                \ <loop> save jump target address
\ kkf@  H@!H@C+H!   \ compile 'k' - read next char
\ klf@  H@!H@C+H!   \ compile 'l' - push literal
\ k:k0- H@!H@C+H!   \ compile 10 (0xa) (':' - '0' = '\n')
\ k=f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ compile '=' - compare with newline
\ kjf@  H@!H@C+H!   \ compile 'j' - jump if zero
\       H@!H@C+H!   \ compile <loop> address
\ k}f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ compile '}' - end definition
\ L!                \ set latest to this word's address

\ , ( x -- ) "comma"
\ Store cell x 'here'.
\ Since 'here' is always cell-aligned, subsequent executions of ',' will store
\ data at cell-intervals one after the other.
H@ L@H@!H@C+H! k1k0-H@. k,H@k1k0-+.H@C+H!
k{f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ begin definition
                  \ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
                  \ store x 'here':
kHf@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( x -- H )          push variable 'here'
k@f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( x H -- x H@ )     fetch address H@ in variable H
k!f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( x H@ -- )         store x in H@
                  \ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
                  \ increment 'here' by 1 cell:
kHf@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( -- H )            push variable 'here'
k@f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( H -- H@ )         fetch address H@ in variable H
kCf@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( H@ -- H@ u )      push cell size u
k+f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( H@ u -- H@+u )    add address H@ with u
kHf@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( H@+u -- H@+u H )  push variable 'here'
k!f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ ( H@+u H -- )       store address H@ in 'here'
                  \ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
k}f@  H@!H@C+H!   \ end definition
L!

\ ' ( "<spaces>c" -- xt ) "tick"
\ Find execution token xt for the next character c in the input stream.
\ Aborts execution if an execution token cannot be found for c.
\ 
\ Compiled structure:
\   J <start>           \ jump to <start>
\   <abort>: l (-11) Q  \ abort with -11 (undefined word) if not found
\   <start>: k f "      \ read char, find xt, dup
\   j <abort>           \ if zero, jump to <abort>
\ 
\ We don't know <start> when compiling J, so we:
\   1. Compiled J with placeholder, save placeholder address
\   2. Save <abort> address after compiling Q
\   3. Patch placeholder with current here (now <start>)
\   4. Compile j, then the saved <abort> address
\ 
H@ L@, k1k0-H@. k'H@k1k0-+.H@C+H!
k{f@,
  kJf@, H@ k0k0-,   \ compile J + placeholder; save placeholder addr
  H@                \ save <abort> addr
  klf@,
  k0k;-,            \ -11 = '0' - ';'
  kQf@,
  H@ < !            \ patch placeholder with <start>
  kkf@,
  kff@,
  k"f@,
  kjf@, ,           \ compile j + <abort> addr
k}f@,
L!

\ c ( "<spaces>c" -- a-addr )
\ Read next character c and begin creating a new definition at addr.
\ This will only fill in the dictionary prologue. You will need to end the
\ definition and update 'latest' yourself.
H@ L@, k1k0-H@. kcH@k1k0-+.H@C+H!
'{,
  'H,'@, 'L,'@,
  'l,k1k0-, 'H,'@,'.,         \ store name length 0x1 here
  'k, 'H,'@,'l,k1k0-,'+, '.,  \ read c and store it at next byte after 0x1
  'H,'@,'l,C,'+,'H,'!,
'},
L!

\ vim:ft=forth
